  "full_response": "In operating systems, **processes** and **threads** are both mechanisms for executing programs, but they differ in key ways:\n\n### **1. Definition**\n- **Process**:\n  - A **process** is an instance of a program in execution.\n  - It includes the program code, data, resources (like file handles, memory), and execution state.\n  - Each process runs in its own **isolated memory space** (virtual address space).\n\n- **Thread**:\n  - A **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process.\n  - Multiple threads can exist within a single process, sharing the same memory and resources.\n  - Threads are sometimes called **\"lightweight processes\"** because they have lower overhead than processes.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Key Differences**\n\n| Feature               | Process                          | Thread                          |\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| **Memory Space**      | Separate (isolated)              | Shared within the same process  |\n| **Creation Overhead** | High (requires new memory allocation) | Low (uses existing process memory) |\n| **Communication**     | Inter-Process Communication (IPC) (slow) | Direct (fast, via shared memory) |\n| **Context Switching** | Slower (requires OS intervention) | Faster (handled within the process) |\n| **Independence**      | Fully independent                | Dependent (if process crashes, all threads die) |\n| **Resource Usage**    | More (each has its own memory)   | Less (shares memory with other threads) |\n| **Parallelism**       | Can run on different CPUs        | Can run on different CPUs (if multi-core) |\n| **Security**          | More secure (isolated)           | Less secure (shared memory risks) |\n\n---\n\n### **3. When to Use Which?**\n- **Use Processes when:**\n  - You need **strong isolation** (e.g., running untrusted code).\n  - You want **fault tolerance** (one process crashing doesn’t affect others).\n  - You need **different memory spaces** (e.g., different user sessions).\n\n- **Use Threads when:**\n  - You need **fast communication** between tasks (e.g., web servers handling multiple requests).\n  - You want **lower overhead** (e.g., background tasks in an app).\n  - You need **parallelism** (e.g., multi-core CPU utilization).\n\n---\n\n### **4.",
